{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "sentiment_driver",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.55,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "A categorical driver axis (service, food, price, ambiance, wait, cleanliness) is exactly the kind of grouping that supports 'how to improve satisfaction'. However, several samples show questionable readings: YLP-0003 is dominated by pizza/food quality and price yet labelled service_quality; YLP-0005 (gave away cabana, denied entry, bad drinks — clearly service/discrimination) is labelled 'other'. The dimension is sound but per-row assignment is unreliable, lowering groundedness."
    },
    {
      "name": "problem_mentioned",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.75,
      "source_groundedness": 0.55,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "A binary problem flag is useful for isolating actionable negative feedback. But labelling is inconsistent: YLP-0005 lists an explicit 'Cons' section with multiple problems yet is coded 0, and YLP-0003 has a stated complaint (smallest pie too big) coded 0. The flag partly aligns with label_pos, giving some redundancy risk, but it captures problems even in positive reviews, so it adds leverage. Groundedness weakened by mislabels."
    },
    {
      "name": "improvement_keyword_count",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.4,
      "source_groundedness": 0.6,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "A raw keyword count is a fairly generic surface signal; it is heavily zero (166/250) and its semantics are vague ('improvement keyword' undefined), limiting analytical leverage for prioritising improvements versus the richer sentiment_driver column. Values are broadly derivable from text (word matching), so groundedness is moderate, though the count for YLP-0002 (=2) is not clearly traceable."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.7,
  "source_groundedness": 0.57,
  "rationale": "The columns provide genuine analytical axes for the improvement query — a driver category and a problem flag are directly relevant for grouping and prioritising fixes, while the keyword count is a weaker generic signal. The main weakness is groundedness: several sampled rows show driver/problem labels that contradict or ignore clear cues in the source text (e.g., food/price-focused review tagged service_quality, an explicit Cons list flagged as no problem), indicating unreliable per-row extraction rather than invented facts."
}

